        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 711,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 646,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the exponentially scaled modified Bessel function of order 1 - I1(x) times e to the minus |x| - to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is I1 for large inputs, where I1 itself would overflow; the ratio I1 / I0 is i1e / i0e and stays finite.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n0 at 0 and antisymmetric, rising to a peak of about 0.219 at x = 1.55, then falling slowly back towards 0 (0.12 at 10).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.i1e\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined and finite for every real number.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-10 to 10 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is I1e(x) of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -10 to 10, drawn from -0.25 (bottom) to 0.25 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.i1 is the unscaled function, t.special.i0e the order-0 version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 630,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the exponentially scaled modified Bessel function of order 1 - I1(x) times e to the minus |x| - to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is I1 for large inputs, where I1 itself would overflow; the ratio I1 / I0 is i1e / i0e and stays finite.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n0 at 0 and antisymmetric, rising to a peak of about 0.219 at x = 1.55, then falling slowly back towards 0 (0.12 at 10).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.i1e\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined and finite for every real number.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-10 to 10 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is I1e(x) of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -10 to 10, drawn from -0.25 (bottom) to 0.25 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.i1 is the unscaled function, t.special.i0e the order-0 version."
        },
